## Goal
DEV-47 "Ask target": for links that no rule can assign to a profile (meetings opened from Calendar or Slack), show a profile picker panel; "Remember" appends a rule to `rules.yaml`. This commit records the design decision as ADR 0005, status proposed.

## Problems
- Rule targets are compared by value (`open != Target{}`), so adding a slice directly to `Target` would break that check.
- Writing `rules.yaml` exists only inside the serve HTTP handler, guarded by an in-process mutex. That does not protect against the link-handler app writing concurrently.
- The decision log has a fixed 5-column format, and the log reader skips lines with any other column count.
- Cocoa must run on the main OS thread, but nothing locks the thread, which probably explains an intermittent lost first link on cold start.
- A lost Slack Meet link on 2026-11-03 was not Portero's fault; Slack never passed it to Launch Services.

## Decision
Write the ADR as proposed only, with no code. It changes the rules format and makes the link handler a second writer of the rules file, so it needs user approval before merge. The ADR covers:
- a separate `Open` type with an `ask` list
- a shared `internal/rulesfile` package with atomic write, hash check and flock
- a native panel in the app
- an optional 6th log column
- the CLI opening the default without asking

## Rejected
None recorded in this slice. Open points for the user to challenge:
- placing the remembered rule before the group instead of inside it
- running panels in parallel
- the CLI not asking

